CONTEXTUAL CONTRADICTIONS AND WHOLE-PAIR TRANSLATION

Lexically shared text does not automatically have the same meaning in two complete
sentences. For a genuine conflict, preserve each side's honest coverage status.
Example: A says "The complete list contains only Ada and Ben." B says "The complete
list contains only Ada, Ben, and Chen." Both claims are contradicted. A's exclusion
can be grounded in its shared "complete ... only Ada ... Ben" span, paired with
B's unique "Chen" difference. Use MEMBERSHIP / CONTRADICTS, with those exact A/B
witnesses in hard_conflict. B's inclusion uses B's unique difference and A's context.
Do not invent an A-only ID when none exists. Do not mark A COVERED just to fit the
old own-unique-only restriction. Do not cite only the identical shared prefix on
B as proof of the differing membership: the added unique item is essential evidence.

This exception is for actual contradictions, never ordinary missing content.
An open list of examples plus another example, an independent article Y, or harmless
chrome is NOT an exclusive-membership conflict. Shared cookie/navigation/disclaimer
text still cannot establish substantive X or prove a missing substantive proposition.
Use the complete original context to decide; ID kinds alone do not decide semantics.

translation_status is a WHOLE-PAIR assertion, not an assertion about just the core X.
If German A translates English B's core policy but B ALSO gives a retained service
offer/contact channel that A lacks, use OTHER and the honest one-way coverage result.
Do not output COMPLETE_FAITHFUL merely because the shared core is faithfully translated.
If ALL retained meanings are preserved in both directions, complete translation can
still be COMPLETE_FAITHFUL and public materiality NONE. Harmless UI is not retained Y.

The side branches keep exactly the same 5/8/4 keys. Recheck translation_status after
both coverage sides: ANY UNCOVERED side rules out COMPLETE_FAITHFUL. Always return
one complete object inside a ```json code fence, including on repair attempts.
